<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wilhelmus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">between <a href="/years/1568">1568</a> and <a href="/years/1572">1572</a>; first attested <a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Wilhelmus van Nassouwe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Wilhelmus&quot; was written at the start of the Eighty Years' War, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, at some time between April 1568 and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brielle">Brielle</a> on 1 April 1572, an event it does not mention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelmus.jpg" alt="Wilhelmus" width="330" height="274" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Handschrift_Brussel_p-37-38.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The melody of the Wilhelmus is a contrafactum of a Catholic French song, Autre chanson de la ville de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> assiégée par le prince de Condé, which mocked the failed siege of Chartres in 1568 by the Huguenot prince the Prince of Condé. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> text replaced the ridicule of one Protestant prince with a tribute to another, William of Orange. The earliest known notation of the tune dates from 1574, when it was still sung at a rapid, military pace.</p><p>The authorship of the words is uncertain. Soon after the song appeared it was ascribed either to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philips">Philips</a> of Marnix, Lord of Saint-Aldegonde, or to the politician Dirck Coornhert, but neither ever claimed it, and some odd rhymes are taken as a sign that an experienced poet did not write it. The oldest surviving complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> text is in a Geuzenliedboek printed in 1577–1578.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Wilhelmus is a monologue in the first person, spoken by William of Nassau, who describes how he tries to stay faithful to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> while serving God and his conscience. Its first stanza presents his quarrel as one with the king's emissaries in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a>, rather than with the king himself. The text has 15 stanzas, and their first letters form the acrostic Willem van Nassov, a contemporary spelling of Nassau.</p><p>The stanzas are arranged symmetrically, so that the first and fifteenth resemble one another in meaning, as do the second and fourteenth, and so on towards the eighth. That stanza compares William to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> fleeing the tyranny of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saul">King Saul</a>, and the Spanish crown to Saul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> the song served as a political and military signal. It was sung by a soldier during the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haarlem">Haarlem</a> in 1573 and played mockingly by Spanish musicians at the siege of Alkmaar the same year. In the 17th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> used it at sea and in colonial wars, and in 1626 Adriaen Valerius published a slower, solemn version for singing at home to the lute. In the 18th century it became a quick Orangist march, the Prinsenmars, and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart</a> wrote seven variations on it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1766.</p><p>Wilhelmina asked for the Wilhelmus at her inauguration in 1898, and on 10 May 1932 it replaced the older song. During the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was taken up by all factions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> resistance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wilhelmus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sukarno found his first recording, made in early 1951 with the orchestras of Radio Republik Indonesia, too ornate and asked for something as plain as the red and white flag and as grave as the Dutch anthem &quot;<b>Wilhelmus</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li><li><blockquote>…was brought into Great Britain as a military march in the reign of William III (1689–1702) and resembles one written for Prince John William of Friesland; Henry Grattan Flood proposed another ancestor, the Dutch march &quot;<b>Wilhelmus</b> van Nassouwe&quot; of 1672, itself reworked from a French version of 1568.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-british-grenadiers">The British Grenadiers</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Wilhelmus</b>&quot; was written at the start of the Eighty Years' War, the Dutch Revolt against the Spanish Empire, at some time between April 1568 and the capture of Brielle on 1 April 1572, an event it does not mention.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a></li></ul></section>
